 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak. The price level is now far higher than it was a few years ago.**\n\n**Annual rent inflation (ABS CPI, Sydney)**\n- It peaked at 8.8% in March 2024.\n- It was 6.0% in December 2024 and 3.2% in August 2025.\n- In August 2026 it was 3.5%.\n- Since late 2025 it has sat at 3.3–3.5%. The fall has stopped, and it is no longer easing.\n\n**How much higher rents are**\n- The Sydney rent index rose from 114.0 in Q4 2021, the post-COVID low, to 139.5 in Q3 2025. That is about 22%.\n- The monthly series (September 2025 = 100) rose from 82.6 in July 2022 to 103.3 in August 2026. That is about 25% in four years.\n- All-items CPI in Sydney rose about 18% from Q4 2021 to Q3 2025, so rents have risen faster than prices generally.\n- Sydney rents were falling slightly in 2020–21 (119.0 in Q1 2020 to 114.0 in Q4 2021). The 2022–24 surge came on top of that dip.\n\n**Compared with other capitals (annual rent inflation, August 2026)**\n\n| City | Annual rent inflation |\n|---|---|\n| Perth | 5.3% |\n| Brisbane | 4.7% |\n| Adelaide | 4.1% |\n| Sydney | 3.5% |\n| Australia (eight-capital average) | 3.6% |\n| Melbourne | 2.5% |\n\nSydney is close to the national average. Melbourne is the cooling city, while Perth, Brisbane and Adelaide are still climbing faster than Sydney.\n\n**Overall**\n- The rent shock of 2022–24 is not being reversed. Rents are not falling, so tenants are not getting relief on the higher level.\n- The pace is now moderate. At 3.5%, rent inflation is roughly in line with overall inflation. Sydney's all-items index rose about 3.6% over the past 12 months, using the monthly index levels.\n- The CPI measures rents paid by all tenants, including those on older leases. It lags what a new renter sees on listings, so asking rents and vacancy rates may look tougher than this. I haven't pulled those figures.",
